    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        553,
        294.3668144843573
      ],
      "size": [
        759.984375,
        271.34375
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Omnidirectional cameras (CMei's model). A catadioptric rig - a camera pointed at a curved mirror - sees PAST 180 degrees, which no pinhole and no fisheye model can express, so cv2.omnidir adds a mirror parameter 'xi' on top of K and four (k1, k2, p1, p2) coefficients. Three things this workflow shows. (1) The calibration fits these eight views to 0.05 px - and still reports fx 327 / xi 0.96 for a camera whose truth is 300 / 0.80. Focal length and xi are strongly correlated, so a low RMS says the parameter SET is self-consistent, not that either number is right; only boards spread into the periphery separate them. The principal point does come out right (399.9 against 400). (2) There is no single 'undistorted' image: the view is wider than a plane holds, so you pick a projection. The four 'Omnidir Undistort' instances render the SAME frame as a flat photo of the middle, two unrolled panorama strips and a conformal stereographic view. Note the two unrolled modes need a width/height-over-pi camera matrix, which the node builds for you - a perspective-style K there renders black and cv2 says nothing. (3) The bottom half reconstructs depth from an omnidir STEREO pair in one call: rectifying a catadioptric pair is not a homography, so cv2.omnidir.stereoReconstruct does rectify + SGBM + back-projection together. It is fed the known camera parameters as literals, on purpose - the two halves are independent, so a calibration miss cannot take the depth half down. The last row calibrates blank frames: no board, found=false, no raise."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
